			<view isee:template_view="template:11" isee:name="Static Insight 1" isee:original_design="Deluxe Prev-Next Blue Green Pastel" background="white" page_width="1066" page_height="600" type="interface">
				<style>
					<isee:stacked_container item_spacing="0" corner_radius="4" selected_item_color="black" selected_item_background="white" item_background="#DDDDDD" transparent_bg="false"/>
				</style>
				<text_box z_index="7" background="#C9D2D8" font_weight="bold" text_align="right" vertical_text_align="bottom" font_color="#284B63" font_size="28pt" border_color="#3D405B" border_width="thick" vertical_scrollbar="true" appearance="transparent" x="27.5327" y="104.304" width="387.881" height="50.5037">CHP resourcing results</text_box>
				<text_box z_index="7" background="#C9D2D8" font_color="#3D405B" font_size="14pt" padding="5" border_color="#3D405B" border_width="thick" vertical_scrollbar="true" appearance="transparent" x="101.54" y="177.669" width="395.249" height="362.569">CHP productivity will decline and plateau with no further education investments.

All-Kenya average - recruitment rate of 2% per annum will not close current demand gap (workload profile shifting from under-5s to over-65s)
OR
2% per annum with 15 hours supervision/training will close gap by 2040.

Subcounties with largest demand gaps: Shinyalu, Turbo (&gt;50%).
Subcounties with smallest demand gaps: Mumias West, Soy, Suguta. Note poverty assumptions drive demand assumptions.

Training need is universal. Recruitment policy must be tailored to subcounty demand patterns. 

All scenarios assume 50% supply availability.</text_box>
