			<view isee:template_view="template:11" isee:name="Static Insight 2" isee:original_design="Deluxe Prev-Next Blue Green Pastel" background="white" page_width="1066" page_height="600" type="interface">
				<style>
					<isee:stacked_container item_spacing="0" corner_radius="4" selected_item_color="black" selected_item_background="white" item_background="#DDDDDD" transparent_bg="false"/>
				</style>
				<text_box z_index="7" background="#C9D2D8" font_weight="bold" text_align="right" vertical_text_align="bottom" font_color="#284B63" font_size="28pt" border_color="#3D405B" border_width="thick" vertical_scrollbar="true" appearance="transparent" x="106.763" y="78.9195" width="395.573" height="80.5037">CHP unmet demand, supplies and trust results</text_box>
				<text_box z_index="7" background="#C9D2D8" font_color="#3D405B" font_size="14pt" padding="5" border_color="#3D405B" border_width="thick" vertical_scrollbar="true" appearance="transparent" x="102.31" y="173.742" width="395.249" height="364.876">Trust in CHPs is built and maintained through reliable delivery of services.
Supply constraints affect CHP performance through 
- inability to provide some services, increasing demand gaps 
- reduction of trust in CHPs

Modelled scenarios show how the demand gap and trust gap reduces to close the CHP resources gap when supplies are available. Addressing this resources gap through recruitment and training gives optimum service availability.

Individual historical results alignment indicate that average supply availability has been no more than 50%. 

Supply availability also has an effect on CHP motivation, creating a virtuous circle of trust and adequate supplies increasing morale (and vice versa).

Note that trust is initialised at an average level; subcounty patterns cannot be seen due to lack of data.

</text_box>
				<graphics_frame fill="none" x="507.245" y="104.846" width="579.678" height="412.308">
